---
// firma2-project-timeline — the project's milestones, oldest first: the dated
// events a program manager uses to answer "where is this in its life?" without
// reading the whole record. Scoping and permitting, then construction, then the
// monitoring that outlives it.
//
// bcn-lego-checked: there is NO timeline lego. `ls node_modules/@esa/ecology/src/
// components/` (67 files, checked 2026-08-20) has no esa-timeline, esa-stepper,
// esa-steps or esa-progress-list; esa-progress-bar is a single ratio, not a dated
// sequence, and esa-pagination's step dots are a control, not a record. Beacon is
// not cloned on this machine, so tier 2 could not be walked. What IS composed from
// legos is everything inside the shell: esa-card is the section surface, esa-button
// (chrome/iconOnly — esa-icon-button is a deprecated shim over it) is the header
// affordance, and esa-icon draws all three status markers. The bespoke part is
// exactly one thing — the three-column subgrid that keeps the date column aligned
// down the list — and no layouts.css primitive can do that: `.cluster` and `.repel`
// are flex, so every row would size its own date and the column would go ragged,
// and `.grid` is an auto-fit track generator for equal cards, not a row template.
// firma2-project-timeline is the reusable home for it (see the note at the bottom
// on promoting it to the hub).
import EsaCard from '@esa/ecology/esa-card.astro';
import '../shared/firma2-card-section.css';
import EsaButton from '@esa/ecology/esa-button.astro';
import EsaIcon from '@esa/ecology/esa-icon.astro';
import type { Milestone } from '../../data/firma2-project-detail';

interface Props {
  /** Chronological, oldest first — the order buildMilestones() emits. */
  milestones: Milestone[];
}

const { milestones } = Astro.props;

// One marker per status. Three deliberate choices are packed in here:
//
// SHAPE, NOT A COLOURED EDGE. design-principles bans `border-left: Npx solid
// <color>` as a status indicator by name, and a vertical timeline is the exact
// place that anti-pattern shows up — a tinted rail segment per row. These are
// icons instead, so the state survives a greyscale print and a colour-blind
// reader. All three sit on the same 24-unit ring, which is why they line up in a
// single-pixel-wide column and the rail can run dead straight between them.
//
// `paths` IS THE LEGO'S OWN EXTENSION POINT, not a fork. esa-icon documents it as
// "inner SVG markup for a glyph not in the built-in registry", used as the
// fallback when `name` is unknown. ICON_PATHS ships circle-check but neither
// `circle` nor `circle-dot` (checked icon-registry.ts, 55 glyphs), so those two
// carry their Lucide bodies inline. `name` is still passed for both: the registry
// wins when it has the glyph, so the day the hub adds them this file needs no edit.
//
// THE LABEL IS THE ACCESSIBLE HALF. With `label` set, esa-icon renders
// role="img" + aria-label, so the status is text to a screen reader and not
// colour alone (SC 1.4.1) or shape alone (SC 1.1.1). It is announced BEFORE the
// date and title because the DOM order is marker, date, title — "Complete,
// February 2024, Site assessment and planting plan complete" is the order a
// person reads the row in too.
const MARKERS: Record<Milestone['status'], { name: string; paths?: string; label: string }> = {
  complete: {
    name: 'circle-check',
    label: 'Complete',
  },
  current: {
    name: 'circle-dot',
    paths: '<circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"></circle><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="1"></circle>',
    label: 'In progress',
  },
  upcoming: {
    name: 'circle',
    paths: '<circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"></circle>',
    label: 'Upcoming',
  },
};

// NO EMPTY STATE ON PURPOSE. buildMilestones() throws when a program has no
// template and otherwise always returns six, so an empty list is unreachable in
// this prototype — an esa-empty-state here would be a string nobody can ever see,
// and polish is subtractive. If real data ever arrives that can be empty, that is
// the moment to add one, with copy written for whatever "empty" turns out to mean.
---
<EsaCard title="Milestones">
  <!-- ADD, not edit. This section is a COLLECTION, and that is what separates
       it from Basics and Description: those hold scalar values, so each one got
       a per-field affordance that appears on hover and edits in place, and their
       section pencils went away. A set has no single field to hover — you add to
       it, remove from it, reorder it — so the section keeps one control and it
       names the act it actually performs. "Edit <section>" was the old pencil's
       label and it was vague about which of those it meant.

       `size="sm"` is not a local taste call — it is the size every section's
       header control renders at, and the card headers down this page have to
       agree exactly in height. Change it here only if it changes in all of them.

       Still inert: nothing is wired behind it yet. It is a real, enabled
       <button> so the eventual handler has somewhere to land, and deliberately
       not `disabled` — disabled claims the action is unavailable to THIS user,
       which is a different and false statement. Per-row editing and removal
       inside this section are not built yet. -->
  <EsaButton slot="actions" variant="chrome" size="sm" icon="plus">Add milestone</EsaButton>

  <!-- <ol>, because these are sequential and countable and the semantics are
       free. role="list" is not redundant belt-and-braces: Safari + VoiceOver drop
       list semantics from any list whose `list-style` is none, and the numbers
       have to go — the markers already number the rows visually. -->
  <ol class="firma2-timeline" role="list">
    {milestones.map((milestone) => {
      const marker = MARKERS[milestone.status];
      return (
        <li class="firma2-timeline__item" data-status={milestone.status}>
          <span class="firma2-timeline__marker">
            <EsaIcon name={marker.name} paths={marker.paths} size="sm" label={marker.label} />
          </span>
          <span class="firma2-timeline__date typography-body-md">{milestone.date}</span>
          <span class="firma2-timeline__title typography-body-md">{milestone.title}</span>
        </li>
      );
    })}
  </ol>
</EsaCard>

<style>
  /* Row gap lives on the CELLS, not on the grid, and that is load-bearing rather
     than a preference: the rail below is a flex child of the marker column that
     grows to fill its row, so it can only reach the next marker if the row's
     height includes the spacing. A `row-gap` would open a hole the rail cannot
     cross without a negative margin. Paying for it with cell padding keeps the
     rail continuous and the arithmetic honest. */
  .firma2-timeline {
    display: grid;
    /* marker | date | title. The date is `max-content` ONCE for the whole list,
       which is the entire reason this is a grid: every date starts at the same x,
       so the column scans vertically. minmax(0, 1fr) rather than 1fr so a long
       milestone title wraps instead of forcing the track wider. */
    grid-template-columns: max-content max-content minmax(0, 1fr);
    column-gap: var(--spacing-300, 0.75rem);
    row-gap: 0;
    /* <ol> arrives with UA margin, padding-inline-start for the numbers, and a
       list-style this list does not use — the markers ARE the numbering. */
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
  }

  /* subgrid is what makes the shared date column possible while each <li> stays a
     real box — needed because the rail is drawn inside one. `display: contents`
     would also share the tracks but would delete the box and with it the rail. */
  .firma2-timeline__item {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: subgrid;
    grid-column: 1 / -1;
  }

  .firma2-timeline__item:not(:last-child) > .firma2-timeline__date,
  .firma2-timeline__item:not(:last-child) > .firma2-timeline__title {
    padding-block-end: var(--spacing-400, 1rem);
  }

  .firma2-timeline__marker {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: center;
    /* The 16px icon optically centres on the first line of a 16px/1.5 title, not
       on its top edge. A quarter-step is the difference; anything token-driven is
       coarser than the correction. */
    padding-block-start: var(--spacing-100, 0.25rem);
  }

  /* The rail. STRUCTURE, NOT STATUS: one neutral hairline in the border role, the
     same colour under a complete milestone as under an upcoming one, because what
     it says is "these are one sequence" and the markers say the rest. Colouring it
     per row is the left-border anti-pattern wearing a costume.

     `:not(:last-child)` is how it stops at the last marker instead of trailing off
     into the card's padding — the final milestone has no next event to connect to.
     flex: 1 lets it grow to whatever height the row's text ended up needing, so a
     title that wraps to three lines does not break the line. */
  .firma2-timeline__item:not(:last-child) > .firma2-timeline__marker::after {
    content: '';
    flex: 1;
    width: var(--border-width-default, 1px);
    margin-block-start: var(--spacing-100, 0.25rem);
    background: var(--color-border-default, #cccfcc);
  }

  /* Tabular figures so the years stack digit over digit down the column. The
     months still vary in width, which is why the grid track — not the font — is
     what actually aligns the column; this just stops "2024" and "2031" from
     drifting against each other inside it. */
  .firma2-timeline__date {
    color: var(--color-content-default-secondary, #60655f);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
    /* A date is one token; wrapping "February" off "2024" would be noise. */
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  /* The title stays at full strength at every status. An upcoming milestone is
     real information — it is the answer to "what happens next" — and greying it
     is the "fainter as it matters less" move design-principles rules out. Its
     quietness comes from the marker's shape instead: an empty ring carries a
     fraction of the ink of a check. */
  .firma2-timeline__title {
    color: var(--color-content-default, #1d211c);
  }

  /* Status colour, on the marker only.
     complete + upcoming share the secondary content role. That is deliberate, not
     an oversight: the obvious "quieter" choice for upcoming is a border step, and
     --color-border-default-strong resolves to olive-8 (#b9bcb8) here — about
     1.9:1 on the card, well under the 3:1 SC 1.4.11 asks of a graphic that
     carries meaning. So the two states separate by GLYPH (check vs empty ring),
     which costs no contrast at all, and neither one is dimmed. */
  .firma2-timeline__item[data-status='complete'] > .firma2-timeline__marker,
  .firma2-timeline__item[data-status='upcoming'] > .firma2-timeline__marker {
    color: var(--color-content-default-secondary, #60655f);
  }

  /* The one place colour is spent. Brand at the content step (grass-11), which is
     a text-legible ramp position rather than the brand FILL — the house keeps
     saturated brand for the primary action, and this is a marker, not a button.
     Paired with circle-dot's filled centre, "now" is the only row on the list
     carrying both a hue and a solid centre, and it needs no tinted row behind it
     to be found. */
  .firma2-timeline__item[data-status='current'] > .firma2-timeline__marker {
    color: var(--color-content-brand, #2a7e3b);
  }
</style>
